title: Relative file references inside a kubeconfig resolve against the cwd, not against
  the file
updated: 2026-08-13T17:35:30Z
---

kubectl resolves every relative path a kubeconfig names -- certificate-authority, client-certificate, client-key, and the exec plugin command when it is a path -- against the directory of the kubeconfig file that names it. Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig passes those strings through untouched (Kubeconfig.pm, _resolve_cert and api), so they end up resolved against the process working directory.

For a single ~/.kube/config with absolute paths, which is what the file kubectl writes contains, this never shows. It shows for a kubeconfig checked into a project next to its certs -- "certificate-authority: ca.crt" works when you run from the project root and breaks from anywhere else -- and it got more visible with #10: a merged KUBECONFIG list draws entries from several directories at once, so there is no single directory the whole configuration is relative to any more, and a relative cert in the second file of the list means something different to kubectl than to us.

Fixing it means remembering, per parsed file, which path it came from, and resolving the *-data-less cert fields against that directorys before they reach Kubernetes::REST::Server. The merge in _merge_configs keeps whole entries from one file, so the file each entry came from is still knowable there -- the resolution has to happen while the file is being read, or the entry has to carry its origin.

This changes the meaning of an existing relative path in an existing kubeconfig, so it is a behaviour change with a Changes bullet, not a silent fix. #10 documents the current cwd-relative behaviour in the MERGING section of Kubeconfig.pm; that paragraph goes away with this.
